+    <section class="journal" aria-labelledby="journal-heading">
+      <p class="journal-kicker">From seed to cup</p>
+      <h2 id="journal-heading">Roaster's Journal</h2>
+      <p class="journal-intro">Good coffee begins long before we light the roaster. Here’s how we choose ours.</p>
+
+      <div class="journal-copy" id="journal-copy">
+        <p>Our bean sourcing process starts with people and a place, not just a flavor on a tasting sheet. We look for growers and cooperatives who can tell us where a coffee was grown, when it was harvested, and how it was processed. Working with sourcing partners who keep that information connected to each lot helps us understand the journey behind every bag. A beautiful cup matters, but so does knowing whose care made it possible.</p>
+
+        <p>Before we choose a coffee, we taste samples side by side in a process called cupping. We brew each sample the same way, then look for sweetness, balance, clarity, and a finish that makes us want another sip. We taste again as the cups cool, because a coffee’s character changes with temperature. Some lots offer bright citrus and floral notes; others bring the comfort of cocoa, toasted nuts, or ripe fruit. We choose coffees for their own character and for the way they will taste in your everyday brew.</p>
+
+        <p>Quality is only one part of the conversation. We ask our sourcing partners about how producers are paid, what challenges they face during the harvest, and which farming practices suit their land. Shade trees, careful water use, and healthy soil are all part of that discussion, though the right approach depends on the farm and its climate. We value clear answers over broad promises. Where we can, we return to the same producers across harvests, building relationships that allow feedback and planning to travel in both directions.</p>
+
+        <p>After harvest, processing shapes the flavor we eventually bring to your cup. In washed coffees, the fruit is removed before the beans are dried; in natural coffees, the fruit stays around the seed during drying. Honey processing sits between those approaches, leaving some sticky fruit material on the bean. We consider the care taken at every stage, including drying, sorting, storage, and transport. Once a selected lot arrives, we compare it with the original sample to check that its quality has held up on the journey.</p>
+
+        <p>At our roastery, we develop a roast profile for each coffee in small test batches. Our aim is to reveal the work already done at origin, balancing sweetness and body without losing the flavors that made us choose the beans. We taste, adjust, and keep notes before a coffee reaches the menu. Because coffee is a seasonal crop, our selection changes with harvests and availability. Those changes are an invitation to discover a new place, a new producer, and another reason to slow down over your next cup.</p>
+      </div>
